  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday Port Washington ran hot and Hicksville ran thin\u2014and the capacity gap between them is your immediate problem.\n\nPort Washington delivered $3,981 on 6 active groomers ($498/groomer, in line with your 30-day baseline), anchored by strong add-on attach (71% vs historical 56%) and a retail spike (+74%). This is real performance: transactions jumped 52% despite flat grooming volume, signaling solid front-of-house execution and customer willingness to buy. Your YTD picture is excellent\u2014up 46% revenue and 46% appointments versus last year. However, your August MTD is down 35% in absolute dollars, but strip out the calendar: DOW-adjusted, you're actually +21.3% versus expectation. The real story is capacity. Port Washington is running 3.3 groomer-days per day in August versus 6.0 in July\u2014a structural staffing cliff. You're still performing per groomer, but you've got half the staff on the floor.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday one groomer ($485/day, above the 30-day $417 baseline) faced a grooming revenue drop of 11% week-over-week. MTD is catastrophic: down 72% in appointments, down 66% in revenue. The DOW math shows this is *not* a calendar miss\u2014it's real. Hicksville is running 1.0 groomer-day per day in August versus 3.7 in July. Both locations have lost grooming capacity month-over-month, but Hicksville has lost the war: you're understaffed to the point of operational failure.\n\nYou have a 469-person lapse pool at Port Washington worth $296K annualized\u2014reachable if capacity returns. Restock X-Small Treat Table and Shanti's Medical Journey across both stores; both are deeply negative.\n\n**Today: 6 groomers Port Washington + 0 bathers, 3 groomers Hicksville + 0 bathers; next 7 days: 52 groomer-days total (34 Port, 18 Hicksville), 43 bather hours (Port only).**"
